WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:06.268
Welcome to CSC-244. In this lesson, you’ll declare typed values in Kotlin, build messages

00:00:06.268 --> 00:00:11.392
with string templates, choose results with conditional expressions, and use ranges and loops

00:00:11.392 --> 00:00:17.698
to repeat a task. These are Kotlin ways to store information, combine text with values, make

00:00:17.698 --> 00:00:19.275
decisions, and repeat work.

00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:26.938
You already know these general ideas from Java or C++. Learning their Kotlin forms will help

00:00:26.938 --> 00:00:33.184
you write app logic that checks user input, reports useful status, and handles repeated work

00:00:33.184 --> 00:00:33.600
clearly.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.573
Imagine you’re developing an Android notes app. Before building its screens, you need to

00:00:39.573 --> 00:00:45.967
check the logic that describes a note. Our sample note has the text title Lab ideas and a

00:00:45.967 --> 00:00:50.940
whole-number revision count. The count records edits and starts at one. We’ll record one

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:51.650
additional edit.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:57.798
The program should report whether the title is empty, choose a message for the revision

00:00:57.798 --> 00:01:03.977
count, and print numbered preview labels. A count of zero means a new note; one means a first

00:01:03.977 --> 00:01:08.782
revision; larger counts need a different message. We’ll use two preview labels to check

00:01:08.782 --> 00:01:09.125
repetition.

00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:14.126
Let’s open demo.kts in the Workspace and build that

00:01:14.126 --> 00:01:14.558
logic.

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:17.690
This Kotlin script can contain statements directly,

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:20.983
so we don’t need a Java-style main method.

00:01:21.233 --> 00:01:24.646
The Run Code button runs this script with the

00:01:24.646 --> 00:01:25.783
installed Kotlin compiler.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.766
First, we need names for the note’s title and its

00:01:33.766 --> 00:01:35.268
changing revision count.

00:01:35.268 --> 00:01:38.523
We’ll keep the title binding fixed and let the count

00:01:38.523 --> 00:01:40.400
change when we record an edit.

00:01:48.965 --> 00:01:51.964
The val keyword prevents assigning a different value

00:01:51.964 --> 00:01:52.713
to noteTitle.

00:01:52.713 --> 00:01:55.827
The var keyword allows revisionCount to be reassigned.

00:01:55.827 --> 00:01:58.768
Kotlin infers String from the text and Int from the

00:01:58.768 --> 00:02:01.594
whole number, so the types are still checked even

00:02:01.594 --> 00:02:04.016
though we haven’t written them explicitly.

00:02:04.016 --> 00:02:06.265
The update records the additional edit.

00:02:06.966 --> 00:02:10.164
With the note’s values stored, we need a useful

00:02:10.164 --> 00:02:12.409
response when its title is empty.

00:02:12.409 --> 00:02:16.015
An if expression can choose text and supply that text

00:02:16.015 --> 00:02:17.716
directly to titleMessage.

00:02:27.769 --> 00:02:30.609
Here, structural equality compares the contents of

00:02:30.609 --> 00:02:33.044
the title with an empty string.

00:02:33.294 --> 00:02:36.777
This differs from using double equals to compare

00:02:36.777 --> 00:02:38.519
object references in Java.

00:02:38.769 --> 00:02:41.836
For an empty title, the condition is true and the

00:02:41.836 --> 00:02:43.369
message is Add a title.

00:02:43.619 --> 00:02:46.632
For the separate example Shopping, the condition is

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:49.269
false and the message is Title ready.

00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:52.519
Only the selected branch supplies the value.

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:56.144
This check detects empty text; it doesn’t reject a

00:02:56.144 --> 00:02:58.019
title made only of spaces.

00:02:58.288 --> 00:03:00.620
The title check handles two choices.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:04.054
Revision status needs several choices, so we’ll use a

00:03:04.054 --> 00:03:07.488
when expression to choose one message from the count.

00:03:20.168 --> 00:03:22.020
Work through two possible counts.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.939
With zero, the first branch matches and supplies New

00:03:24.939 --> 00:03:25.219
note.

00:03:25.219 --> 00:03:28.250
With one, zero doesn’t match; the next branch supplies

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:29.092
First revision.

00:03:29.092 --> 00:03:31.393
Kotlin selects the first matching branch.

00:03:31.393 --> 00:03:34.311
The else branch supplies a value when neither listed

00:03:34.311 --> 00:03:35.097
count matches.

00:03:35.097 --> 00:03:37.791
That gives revisionMessage one selected message,

00:03:37.791 --> 00:03:39.643
rather than running every branch.

00:03:39.946 --> 00:03:41.316
We have the messages.

00:03:41.316 --> 00:03:44.775
To show the note title beside its title status, we’ll

00:03:44.775 --> 00:03:48.299
use a string template, which inserts named values into

00:03:48.299 --> 00:03:48.625
text.

00:03:48.625 --> 00:03:52.084
Then we’ll print the selected revision message on its

00:03:52.084 --> 00:03:52.671
own line.

00:03:59.743 --> 00:04:03.240
The dollar-prefixed names insert their current values

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.758
into the first message.

00:04:04.758 --> 00:04:07.860
The colon and space separate the title from its

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:08.322
status.

00:04:08.322 --> 00:04:11.687
Each println call writes a line, making the results

00:04:11.687 --> 00:04:12.743
easy to inspect.

00:04:13.168 --> 00:04:16.376
The remaining job is to number the previews without

00:04:16.376 --> 00:04:18.012
copying a print statement.

00:04:18.012 --> 00:04:20.843
We’ll visit an integer range with a for loop.

00:04:28.035 --> 00:04:30.055
The range includes both endpoints.

00:04:30.055 --> 00:04:33.262
On each visit, previewNumber holds the current number,

00:04:33.262 --> 00:04:36.113
and the loop body prints that number in a label.

00:04:36.113 --> 00:04:39.024
For a separate range from three through four, the

00:04:39.024 --> 00:04:41.815
labels would be Preview three and Preview four.

00:04:41.815 --> 00:04:43.835
Our range is from one through two.

00:04:44.759 --> 00:04:47.522
Before we run, predict the revision message.

00:04:47.522 --> 00:04:50.725
Start with revisionCount’s initial value, apply the

00:04:50.725 --> 00:04:52.986
update, and trace the when branches.

00:04:52.986 --> 00:04:54.933
Which message will be selected?

00:04:54.933 --> 00:04:57.634
Pause the video and explain your reasoning.

00:05:04.314 --> 00:05:07.218
Let’s run the complete script and compare your

00:05:07.218 --> 00:05:09.364
prediction with the actual output.

00:05:25.413 --> 00:05:28.522
The title is Lab ideas, so the empty-title condition

00:05:28.522 --> 00:05:31.392
is false and the first line reports Title ready.

00:05:31.392 --> 00:05:34.500
The count starts at one and increases by one, making

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:34.740
two.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.789
Neither the zero nor the one branch matches, so the

00:05:37.789 --> 00:05:40.180
else branch supplies Multiple revisions.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:43.050
Finally, the inclusive range visits one and two,

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:44.963
producing the two preview lines.

00:05:45.115 --> 00:05:48.219
We’ve checked the notes app’s language-level logic:

00:05:48.219 --> 00:05:51.445
typed bindings store its state, string templates make

00:05:51.445 --> 00:05:54.671
readable messages, conditional expressions select the

00:05:54.671 --> 00:05:57.775
status, and a range-driven loop repeats the preview

00:05:57.775 --> 00:05:58.201
action.

00:05:58.201 --> 00:06:01.366
The printed result lets us verify those rules before

00:06:01.366 --> 00:06:02.340
adding a screen.

00:06:02.517 --> 00:06:05.269
Now transfer this pattern to a reminder-status

00:06:05.269 --> 00:06:05.748
program.

00:06:05.748 --> 00:06:08.440
Create a title, an editable reminder count, a

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:11.013
conditional message, and numbered previews.

00:06:11.013 --> 00:06:13.346
Test zero, one, and multiple reminders.

00:06:13.346 --> 00:06:16.517
Which binding should stay fixed, which should change,

00:06:16.517 --> 00:06:19.508
and what output would convince you that every case

00:06:19.508 --> 00:06:19.867
works?
